course: dictionary
lesson: 9401
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word spur can mean different things, so the words around it tell you which meaning you need. «The rider pressed his heels into the horse, and the spurs jingled on his boots.» Here a spur is a small metal piece on a boot, used to push a horse to go faster.
explain: «Our coach always spurs the team on, shouting, "Keep going!"» Spurs on means encourages, so here spur is a verb: the coach makes the team try harder. Ask yourself which meaning fits, then try it in the sentence to check.
explain: Geography uses spur in another way. «The young river winds around the interlocking spurs in its steep, V-shaped valley.» A spur is a ridge of high land that sticks out from a hill. The clues are the river and the valley.
fact: A spur is a small metal spike or wheel on a rider's boot, used to push a horse to go faster.
fact: «On the spur of the moment» means suddenly, without planning.
fact: To spur someone on is to encourage them to try harder or go faster.
fact: In geography, a spur is a ridge of high land that sticks out from a hill or mountain into a valley.
fact: Spurs is also the nickname of Tottenham Hotspur football club and the San Antonio Spurs basketball team.
exercise: «The rider pressed his heels into the horse, and the spurs jingled on his boots.» In this sentence, what is a spur? | A long, thin river | A soft blanket for the horse | *A small metal piece on a boot, used to push a horse | A type of bird's nest
exercise: «Our coach always spurs the team on at every match.» What does spurs on mean? | Slows them down | Sings a song loudly | *Encourages them to try harder | Sends them home early
exercise: «On the spur of the moment, we decided to go to the beach.» What does on the spur of the moment mean? | In the middle of the night | After months of careful planning | At the very end of the day | *Suddenly, without planning
test: In geography, what is a spur? | *A ridge of high land that sticks out into a valley | A kind of river fish | A flat, empty plain | A deep hole in a cave
test: «Male platypuses have a venomous spur on each back ankle.» What can the spur do to a person? | Keep them warm | *Cause terrible pain | Make them laugh | Help them climb a tree
exam: Which sentence uses spur as a verb? | «He wore sharp spurs on his leather boots.» | *«The teacher's kind words spurred her on to run faster.» | «A rooster has a sharp spur on its leg.» | «The spur of rock stood high above the valley.»
